// Class B, and a different defect from the two-line one above: here the
// reference's output does not merely carry something extra, it fails to LINK.
//
// `fmt` is never called. The reference keeps the method's body in the artefact
// and drops the prelude method that body calls, so the emitted C contains one
// call to `StringBuilder__push_str` and no definition of it. Compiling the file
// alone succeeds - the failure is at link, and under `-Wall` it is an implicit
// declaration first.
//
// The port's demand fixpoint sees the call inside the retained body, materialises
// the callee, links and runs.
struct P { x: int }

impl Display for P {
    fn fmt(*const self, out: *StringBuilder) { out.push_str("x="); }
}

fn main() {
    let p = P { x: 5 };
    println(p.x);
}
